%N A402349 Numbers that are neither squarefree, prime powers, nor highly composite.
%C A402349 Apart from {1, 2, 4, 6}, highly composite numbers are neither squarefree nor prime powers (in A126706).
%C A402349 Numbers k that are neither squarefree nor prime powers such that there exists another number m < k with at least as many divisors as k has.
%C A402349 Superset of A367268, in turn a superset of A368089.
%H A402349 Michael De Vlieger, <a href="/A402349/b402349.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A402349 {a(n)} = A126706 \ A002182.
%F A402349 A002182 = Union of {1, 2, 4, 6} and A126706 \ {a(n)}.
%e A402349 Let d = A000005 and let h be the largest highly composite number that does not exceed a(n).
%e A402349 Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..12:
%e A402349    n  a(n)  d(a(n))   d(h)
%e A402349   ------------------------------
%e A402349    1   18      6      d(12) =  6
%e A402349    2   20      6      d(12) =  6
%e A402349    3   28      6      d(24) =  8
%e A402349    4   40      8      d(36) =  9
%e A402349    5   44      6      d(36) =  9
%e A402349    6   45      6      d(36) =  9
%e A402349    7   50      6      d(48) = 10
%e A402349    8   52      6      d(48) = 10
%e A402349    9   54      8      d(48) = 10
%e A402349   10   56      8      d(48) = 10
%e A402349   11   63      6      d(60) = 12
%e A402349   12   68      6      d(60) = 12
%t A402349 nn = 200; r = 4; s = Select[Range[nn], Nor[SquareFreeQ[#], PrimePowerQ[#] ] &]; Reap[Do[k = s[[i]]; If[# > r, r = #, Sow[k]] &[DivisorSigma[0, k] ], {i, Length[s] }] ][[-1, 1]]
%Y A402349 Cf. A000005, A002182, A013929, A024619, A126706, A367268, A368089.
